WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.120
"Fenugreek Benefits for Sexual Function,
Painful Periods, and Milk Production"

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:15.600
In my video, the "Benefits of
Fenugreek Seeds," I profiled this study

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.160
in which young men were randomized
to a sugar pill or fenugreek capsules

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.560
for eight weeks and got a significant
improvement in upper body strength,

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:26.960
lower body strength,
and body composition,

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:30.160
a significant reduction
in body fat percentage.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:32.000
And the only side effect?

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.320
It can make your sweat and
pee smell like maple syrup.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.640
Seems like a bonus!

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.760
Studies on immature castrated rats
suggest the fenugreek muscle

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.840
bulking is a testosterone effect,

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:51.280
but we didn't know what
happens in humans...until now.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:55.440
Four randomized controlled
trials put it to the test and

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.880
saw a significant boost
of total blood testosterone.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.560
And indeed, fenugreek appears
to improve sexual function in men,

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.080
for example, doubling the
frequency of morning erections.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:11.520
What about the sexual function
in women? While the estrogen

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:15.200
hormone, estradiol, stimulates
vaginal lubrication and blood flow,

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.920
facilitating a woman's capacity
for sexual arousal and orgasm,

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.160
it's the testosterone that's
linked with sexual desire

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.320
in both men and women.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.440
Drug companies have tried
testosterone patches on women

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.800
to try to increase sexual desire,
but we're concerned

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.520
about blood clots
and long-term safety.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.600
What about a little fenugreek?

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.800
A significant increase in
testosterone compared to placebo

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:45.600
and a boost in the estrogen estradiol,
which resulted in both an increase

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:49.920
in sexual desire and function,
translating into about a doubling

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.600
of sexual activity
compared to placebo.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:57.200
If fenugreek causes an increase
in estrogen levels, what about

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.600
the efficacy of fenugreek for
reducing menopausal symptoms?

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:06.960
A significant reduction in menopausal
symptoms across every single domain,

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.680
for example, cutting the weekly
numbers of hot flashes and

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:14.609
night sweats in half over
a period of three months.

00:02:14.609 --> 00:02:18.720
Other hormonal effects include
an improvement in painful periods.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:22.400
What I like about this study is that
they didn't use some propriety extract,

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:26.800
but just straight, plain fenugreek
powder that you'd buy at the store.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.600
Now, they packed it into capsules
just so they could pit it against

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.240
a sugar pill placebo,
but the dose they used

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:34.560
is just about a third of
a teaspoon three times a day,

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:36.720
taken the first three
days of their period.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.560
A third of a teaspoon would
cost less than 5 cents.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.680
And boom!
A significant decrease in pain,

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.600
and also appeared to improve
other symptoms as well.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:51.520
While we're on a roll with hormonal
effects, what about the effectiveness

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:57.360
of fenugreek as a galactagogue?
No, not another sci-fi reboot.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.360
A galactagogue is something that
increases breast milk production

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:04.560
in lactating mothers, and
fenugreek fits the bill.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.320
And not just by a little.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:09.680
In this randomized double-blind
placebo-controlled trial in which

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.760
breastfeeding women took less
than a quarter teaspoon of fenugreek,

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.160
less than a quarter teaspoon
of ground ginger,

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.400
and about a 16th of a teaspoon
of turmeric every day,

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.960
doubled their milk production
within a month,

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:28.640
about a 50% increase by week two
and a 100% increase by week four.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.560
However, due to its potential
stimulation on the uterus,

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.720
fenugreek seeds should not
be used during pregnancy.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.280
Though this is all based
on laboratory animal data,

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:41.807
better safe than sorry.

